          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            Just as a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derivator"} derivator
            is a notion which lies in between a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+category"} model category
            or a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} (∞,1)-category
            and its
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+category"} homotopy category
            %em stable
            (or
            = succeed ")" do
              %em triangulated
            %em derivator
            is a notion which lies in between a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stable+model+category"} stable model category
            or a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stable+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} stable (∞,1)-category
            and its homotopy
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/triangulated+category"} triangulated category
          %p
            Stable derivators are a useful refinement of triangulated categories, since they contain enough information so that
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+limit"} homotopy limit
            and colimit constructions can be performed “coherently” and desired maps and objects can be specified by true universal properties. This resolves many common issues with triangulated categories stemming from the fact that at the level of a homotopy category, certain desirable maps can only be stipulated to exist with some weak properties, but not characterized precisely among the class of maps that have those properties.
          %h2#Definition Definition
          %p
            As is the case for
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stable+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-categories"} stable (∞,1)-categories
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stuff%2C+structure%2C+property"} property
            of a derivator rather than a structure (or more precisely, it is a
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/property-like+structure"} property-like structure
            = succeed "-category" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn ∞
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,1)
          %p
            Write
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo □
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \square
            for the “free-living commutative square”
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mover
                            %mo →
                            %mrow
                        %mtd
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %munder
                            %mo →
                            %mrow
                        %mtd
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{ &amp; \overset{}{\to} &amp; \\
                  \\downarrow &amp;&amp; \downarrow\\
                  \&amp; \underset{}{\to} &amp; }
          %p
            and let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi L
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            denote the upper-left
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/span"} span
            and the lower-right
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cospan"} cospan
            as subcategories of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo □
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \square
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi i
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi L
                  %mo →
                  %mo □
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i\colon L\to \square
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi j
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi R
                    %mo →
                    %mo □
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} j \colon R\to \square
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derivator"} derivator
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi D
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi D
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo □
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X\in D(\square)
            is said to be
            %strong cartesian
            if the unit
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mi j
                    %mo *
                  %msup
                    %mi j
                    %mo *
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X\to j_* j^* X
            is an isomorphism, and dually
            %strong cocartesian
            if the counit
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi i
                    %mo !
                  %msup
                    %mi i
                    %mo *
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i_! i^* X\to X
            is an isomorphism. Since
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi i
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi j
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} j
            are fully faithful, it follows from a general theorem about derivators that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi i
                    %mo !
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i_!
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi j
                    %mo *
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} j_*
            are also fully faithful; thus being cartesian is equivalent to being of the form
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi j
                    %mo *
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} j_* Y
            for some
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Y
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi D
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi R
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y\in D(R)
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi i
                      %mo !
                    %mi Z
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i_! Z
          %p
            A derivator
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
            is
            %strong stable
            (or
            = succeed ")" do
              %strong triangulated
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pointed+derivator"} pointed
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi D
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo □
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X\in D(\square)
            is cartesian if and only if it is cocartesian. Such a square is then called
            = succeed "." do
              %strong bicartesian
          %h2#properties Properties
          %h3#triangulation Triangulation
          %p One of the central facts about stable derivators is:
          .un_theorem
            %h6#theorem_maltsiniotis Theorem (Maltsiniotis)
            %p
              If
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi D
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
              is a stable derivator, then each category
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi D
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D(X)
              is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/triangulated+category"} triangulated category
              in a canonical way.
          %p
            We describe the constructions when
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                    %mo =
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X=1
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi D
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mi D
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S\colon D(1)\to D(1)
            is defined by
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                  %mo =
                  %msup
                    %mi b
                    %mo *
                  %msub
                    %mi i
                    %mo !
                  %msub
                    %mi a
                    %mo *
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S = b^* i_! a_*
          %p
            where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mn 1
                  %mo →
                  %mi L
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a\colon 1\to L
            is the inclusion of the top-left vertex of the span, and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi b
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mn 1
                  %mo →
                  %mo □
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b\colon 1\to \square
            is the inclusion of the bottom-right vertex of the square. In other words, for an object
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi D
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X\in D(1)
            its suspension is defined by the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+pushout"} homotopy pushout
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi X
                        %mtd
                          %mover
                            %mo →
                            %mrow
                        %mtd
                          %mn 0
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mn 0
                        %mtd
                          %munder
                            %mo →
                            %mrow
                        %mtd
                          %mi S
                          %mi X
                          %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{X &amp; \overset{}{\to} &amp; 0\\
                  \\downarrow &amp;&amp; \downarrow\\
                  0 &amp; \underset{}{\to} &amp; S X.}
          %p
            This makes sense in the more general context of any
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pointed+derivator"} pointed derivator
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            is actually an equivalence of categories. Its inverse being given by the obvious dual “loop space” construction (which in a general pointed derivator is only right adjoint to it). This provides a motivation for the stability axiom: it is a generalization of the statement that every object is the loop space of its suspension and the suspension of its loop space.
          %p
            One can also prove that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            is also a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/copower"} copower
            with the pointed
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/circle"} circle
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi S
                    %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S^1
            in a suitable sense. In particular, since every object is isomorphic to a double suspension, it is a cogroup object; thus
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi D
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D(X)
            is canonically an
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ab-enriched+category"} Ab-enriched category
          %p
            Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Q
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Q
            denote the category
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mover
                            %mo →
                            %mrow
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo →
                        %mtd
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %munder
                            %mo →
                            %mrow
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo →
                        %mtd
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{ &amp; \overset{}{\to} &amp; &amp; \to &amp;\\
                  \\downarrow &amp;&amp; \downarrow &amp;&amp; \downarrow\\
                  \&amp; \underset{}{\to} &amp; &amp; \to &amp;}
          %p
            with inclusions
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi m
                  %mo ,
                  %mi n
                  %mo ,
                  %mi p
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mo □
                  %mo →
                  %mi Q
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} m,n,p\colon \square \to Q
            of the left and right squares and the outer rectangle, respectively. An object
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi D
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Q
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X\in D(Q)
            is
            %strong bicartesian
            if
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi m
                      %mo *
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} m^*X
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi n
                      %mo *
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n^*X
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi p
                    %mo *
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p^*X
            are all bicartesian.
          %p
            Now consider a bicartesian object
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi D
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Q
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X\in D(Q)
            of the form:
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi A
                        %mtd
                          %mover
                            %mo →
                            %mi f
                        %mtd
                          %mi B
                        %mtd
                          %mo →
                        %mtd
                          %mn 0
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                            %mi g
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mn 0
                        %mtd
                          %mo →
                        %mtd
                          %mi C
                        %mtd
                          %munder
                            %mo →
                            %mi h
                        %mtd
                          %mi D
                          %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{A &amp; \overset{f}{\to} &amp; B &amp; \to &amp; 0\\
                  \\downarrow &amp;&amp; \downarrow^g &amp;&amp; \downarrow\\
                  0 &amp; \to &amp; C &amp; \underset{h}{\to} &amp; D.}
          %p
            In other words, we stipulate that the restrictions to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi D
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D(1)
            along the inclusions of the lower-left and upper-right vertices be zero objects. Now since
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            is bicartesian, the outer square
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi A
                        %mtd
                          %mover
                            %mo →
                            %mrow
                        %mtd
                          %mn 0
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mn 0
                        %mtd
                          %munder
                            %mo →
                            %mrow
                        %mtd
                          %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{A &amp; \overset{}{\to} &amp; 0\\
                  \\downarrow &amp;&amp; \downarrow\\
                  0&amp; \underset{}{\to} &amp; D}
          %p
            is bicartesian, and thus induces an isomorphism
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi D
                    %mo ≅
                    %mi S
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D \cong S A
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            we can extract a “triangle”
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mi f
                  %mi B
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mi g
                  %mi C
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mi h
                  %mi S
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \overset{f}{\to} B \overset{g}{\to} C \overset{h}{\to} S A
          %p
            and we define the
            %em distinguished triangles
            in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi D
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D(1)
            to be those isomorphic to triangles obtained in this way.
          %p One can then prove the axioms of a triangulated category.
          .un_remark
            %h6#remark Remark
            %p
              If
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mi f
                    %mi B
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mi g
                    %mi C
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mi h
                    %mi S
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \overset{f}{\to} B \overset{g}{\to} C \overset{h}{\to} S A
              is a distinguished triangle in a triangulated category, then
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mi f
                    %mi B
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mi g
                    %mi C
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mi h
                    %mi S
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \overset{f}{\to} B \overset{g}{\to} C \overset{-h}{\to} S A
              is also an “exact” triangle in the sense that it induces
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/long+exact+sequences"} long exact sequences
              in
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homology"} homology
              and
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohomology"} cohomology
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi A
                          %mtd
                            %mover
                              %mo →
                              %mi f
                          %mtd
                            %mi B
                          %mtd
                            %mo →
                          %mtd
                            %mn 0
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %msup
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                              %mi g
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mn 0
                          %mtd
                            %mo →
                          %mtd
                            %mi C
                          %mtd
                            %munder
                              %mo →
                              %mi h
                          %mtd
                            %mi D
                            %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\array{A &amp; \overset{f}{\to} &amp; B &amp; \to &amp; 0\\
                    \\downarrow &amp;&amp; \downarrow^g &amp;&amp; \downarrow\\
                    0 &amp; \to &amp; C &amp; \underset{h}{\to} &amp; D.}
            %p
              is bicartesian in an
              = succeed "-category," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn ∞
                      %mo ,
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,1)
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi A
                          %mtd
                            %mover
                              %mo →
                              %mi f
                          %mtd
                            %mi B
                          %mtd
                            %mo →
                          %mtd
                            %mn 0
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %msup
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                              %mi g
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mn 0
                          %mtd
                            %mo →
                          %mtd
                            %mi C
                          %mtd
                            %munder
                              %mo →
                              %mrow
                                %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                                %mi h
                          %mtd
                            %mi D
                            %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\array{A &amp; \overset{f}{\to} &amp; B &amp; \to &amp; 0\\
                    \\downarrow &amp;&amp; \downarrow^g &amp;&amp; \downarrow\\
                    0 &amp; \to &amp; C &amp; \underset{-h}{\to} &amp; D.}
            %p
              This seeming paradox is resolved by noticing that although these two bicartesian diagrams have the same object
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi D
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
              at their lower-right-hand corner, the different maps
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi h
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} h
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mi h
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} -h
              cause these diagrams to induce different isomorphisms
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi D
                      %mo ≅
                      %mi S
                      %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D \cong S A
              %em same
              triangle
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mi f
                    %mi B
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mi g
                    %mi C
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mi h
                    %mi S
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \overset{f}{\to} B \overset{g}{\to} C \overset{h}{\to} S A
              in the homotopy category.
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/triangulated+category"} triangulated category
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pretriangulated+dg-category"}> pretriangulated dg-category
                ,
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/enhanced+triangulated+category"} enhanced triangulated category
            %li
              %p
                %strong stable derivator
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stable+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} stable (∞,1)-category
          %h2#references References
          %p
            See
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derivator"} derivator
            for general references about derivators, and also
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pointed+derivator"} pointed derivator
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Alex+Heller"}> Alex Heller
                , “Stable homotopy theories and stabilization”
                %a{:href => "http://www.ams.org/mathscinet-getitem?mr=1431157"} MR
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Denis-Charles+Cisinski"} Denis-Charles Cisinski
                and Amnon Neeman, “Additivity for derivator K-theory”,
                %a{:href => "http://www.ams.org/mathscinet-getitem?mr=2382732"} MR
            %li
              %p
                Jens Franke,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Uniqueness theorems for certain triangulated categories with an Adams spectral sequence
                %a{:href => "http://www.math.uiuc.edu/K-theory/0139/"} K-theory archive
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Moritz+Groth"}> Moritz Groth
                ,
                %em Derivators, pointed derivators, and stable derivators
                (
                %a{:href => "http://www.math.uni-bonn.de/~mgroth/groth_derivators.pdf"}> pdf
                )
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ian+Coley"}> Ian Coley
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Stabilization of derivators revisited
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1802.04343"} arxiv
